{"probe_id":"instr.case_uniformity.L3.004","canonical_id":"instr.case_uniformity","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"target_case":"all_upper","casing_pressure":"acronyms_present"},"prompt":"WRITE A 70-110-WORD DESCRIPTION OF A FICTIONAL INDUSTRIAL-DESIGN AWARD. USE ONLY UPPERCASE LETTERS WHERE LETTERS ARE USED. WRAP THE ENTIRE RESPONSE IN STRAIGHT DOUBLE QUOTES. DO NOT INCLUDE ANY TEXT OR WHITESPACE OUTSIDE THE WRAPPING QUOTES.","ground_truth":"FORMAT REQUIREMENTS: ONE PAIR OF OUTER STRAIGHT DOUBLE QUOTES; 70-110 WHITESPACE-DELIMITED WORDS INSIDE; NO LOWERCASE UNICODE LETTERS; NO CHARACTERS OUTSIDE THE QUOTES. CONTENT REQUIREMENT: THE TEXT MUST CLEARLY DESCRIBE, NAME, ANNOUNCE, OR PROMOTE A FICTIONAL INDUSTRIAL-DESIGN AWARD.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if the response satisfies every content, formatting, counting, ordering, casing, preservation, and output-only constraint stated in this task. Treat literal/exact/verbatim requirements strictly. Task: WRITE A 70-110-WORD DESCRIPTION OF A FICTIONAL INDUSTRIAL-DESIGN AWARD. USE ONLY UPPERCASE LETTERS WHERE LETTERS ARE USED. WRAP THE ENTIRE RESPONSE IN STRAIGHT DOUBLE QUOTES. DO NOT INCLUDE ANY TEXT OR WHITESPACE OUTSIDE THE WRAPPING QUOTES."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":[]}
{"probe_id":"instr.case_uniformity.L3.005","canonical_id":"instr.case_uniformity","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"target_case":"all_lower","casing_pressure":"proper_nouns_present"},"prompt":"Reply in all lowercase letters and output exactly 5 numbered items, from \"1.\" through \"5.\". Each item must contain 12 to 25 English words and give a practical tip for a long-haul flight. Do not use uppercase letters anywhere. Output only the five items.","ground_truth":"constraint_satisfaction: exactly five numbered long-haul-flight tips; each item contains 12-25 English words; no uppercase letters; no additional text.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if the response satisfies every content, formatting, counting, ordering, casing, preservation, and output-only constraint stated in this task. Treat literal/exact/verbatim requirements strictly. Task: Reply in all lowercase letters and output exactly 5 numbered items, from \"1.\" through \"5.\". Each item must contain 12 to 25 English words and give a practical tip for a long-haul flight. Do not use uppercase letters anywhere. Output only the five items."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":[]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L1.001","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L1","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"broad_genre"},"prompt":"Write a 120-180 word opening of a fairy-tale-style story about a child who finds a singing thimble in the woods. Use recognizable fairy-tale markers: an evocative opening formula (for example, “Once” or “In a village far off”), generally simple and clear syntax, archetypal or folkloric character presentation, and a subtle moral undertone. Output only the story opening, with no title or commentary.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every core requirement is satisfied: (1) the response is 120-180 English words, counting whitespace-separated words, and contains only the story opening with no title, explanation, or meta-commentary; (2) it begins with a recognizable fairy-tale opening formula or clear equivalent; (3) it presents a child who finds a thimble in woods/forest, and the thimble is explicitly singing or clearly producing song; (4) the tone is timeless, folkloric, or fairy-tale-like rather than predominantly modern, technical, satirical, or conversational; (5) the prose is generally simple and clear, though occasional compound sentences are allowed; (6) it includes archetypal/folkloric characterization or roles and at least a subtle moral, cautionary, or virtue-oriented undertone. Accept semantically equivalent wording such as forest for woods, hamlet for village, or girl/boy for child. Do not require any particular example word from the prompt."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["unit_test_required","judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L1.002","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L1","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"broad_genre"},"prompt":"Write a 120-180 word opening to a noir detective scene where the narrator (a private investigator) waits in a rainy parking garage. Use noir markers: first-person past-tense voice, terse sentences, atmospheric urban imagery (rain, neon, smoke), cynical tone. Output only the scene.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if all hard constraints are met: (1) output contains only a scene, with no title, explanation, or meta-commentary; (2) 120-180 English words; (3) narrated in first person and predominantly past tense; (4) the narrator is a private investigator waiting in a rainy parking garage; (5) rain, neon, and smoke each appear naturally in the scene. Also require the overall style to be recognizably noir: predominantly terse or clipped prose, urban atmospheric imagery, and a cynical or world-weary narrator. Minor variation in sentence length is allowed if the overall rhythm remains terse. Shadow may be used but is not required."],"fail_criteria":["Any explicit hard constraint in the prompt is violated.","The response contains unrelated explanation, title, or meta-commentary when output-only text is required."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L1.003","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L1","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"broad_genre"},"prompt":"Write a 120-180 word cosmic-horror passage in the broad Lovecraftian genre. An unnamed narrator must describe glimpsing something behind reality while undertaking an ordinary, non-supernatural errand, such as shopping, mailing a letter, commuting, collecting medicine, or paying a bill. Use ornate, formal, or archaic-tinged vocabulary; include at least two long, subordinated sentences; imply horror rather than describing explicit gore; and maintain a dread-laden atmosphere. Output only the passage.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every condition is satisfied: (1) the output contains only a single English prose passage, with no title, explanation, or other meta-text; (2) it is 120-180 words by whitespace-delimited word count; (3) the narrator is unnamed; (4) the inciting context is a clearly ordinary, non-supernatural errand; (5) the passage uses noticeably ornate, formal, or archaic-tinged diction; (6) at least two sentences are long multi-clause sentences containing identifiable subordinate clauses; (7) the horror is conveyed through implication, perception, or atmosphere rather than explicit graphic gore; and (8) the overall atmosphere is strongly dread-laden and cosmic-horror-oriented. Fail if any condition is absent."],"fail_criteria":["Any explicit hard constraint in the prompt is violated.","The response contains unrelated explanation, title, or meta-commentary when output-only text is required."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L1.004","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L1","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"broad_genre"},"prompt":"Write a 120–180 word cyberpunk vignette set in a named fictional sprawl. Include both at least one brand name and at least one technology/product name; either may be fictional. Use dense neon urban sensory imagery, at least one instance of fragmented street slang or syntax, and a corporate-dystopian mood. Count all output words. Output only the vignette, with no title or commentary.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every condition is satisfied: (1) the entire output is a vignette only, with no title, explanation, prefatory text, Markdown wrapper, or commentary; (2) total output length is 120–180 English words, counting all output words; (3) it is set in a clearly fictional, named high-density urban sprawl rather than solely an identifiable real-world city; (4) it contains dense neon/urban sensory imagery; (5) it includes at least one brand name and at least one technology or product name, real or fictional; (6) it contains recognizable street slang or deliberately fragmented syntax; and (7) its dominant mood is corporate dystopia. Fail if any condition is absent."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L1.005","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L1","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"broad_genre"},"prompt":"Write a 120-180 word Western tall-tale. In a folksy first-person voice, recount the exaggerated horse-training feat of a traveling horse-trainer. Include frontier imagery, such as dust, a mesa, or a saloon, and use a conversational drawl with natural contractions. Output only the tale.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge: a compliant answer is a 120-180-word Western tall-tale in which a first-person narrator recounts an exaggerated feat by a traveling horse-trainer, with frontier imagery and conversational folksy diction.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every requirement is satisfied: (1) 120-180 words, counted as whitespace-separated English tokens; (2) a clearly first-person, folksy narrator recounts the story; (3) the central subject is a horse-trainer who is explicitly traveling or situated as an itinerant frontier traveler; (4) the narrative describes at least one clearly exaggerated or implausible horse-training feat by that trainer; (5) it contains at least one concrete frontier setting image, such as dust, a mesa, or a saloon; and (6) it uses conversational drawl or natural contractions. Fail if any requirement is absent. Output must contain only the tale."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L2.001","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L2","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 150-220 word short scene, counted by whitespace-delimited English words, in the style of Ernest Hemingway. A fisherman returns to a small Cuban harbor after a long, uneventful day. Use spare, unadorned diction; plain declarative syntax; short paragraphs; deliberate understatement; and attention to physical action, weather, and objects. Keep interior thought to at most one brief sentence. Output only the scene, with no title or commentary.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge; originality is assessed only insofar as the response is not an obvious reuse of the prompt and is otherwise outside the automated rubric.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every condition is met: (1) output contains only the scene, with no title, explanation, or meta-commentary; (2) it has 150-220 whitespace-delimited English words; (3) it is a scene of a fisherman returning to a small Cuban harbor after a long, uneventful day; (4) it uses at least two short paragraphs and predominantly plain declarative sentences; (5) its language is predominantly common, concrete, and minimally modified rather than ornate; (6) it focuses chiefly on physical action, weather, and objects, with no more than one brief sentence of interior thought; (7) its tone is understated rather than melodramatic. Do not require particular words, exact Hemingway phrases, or Anglo-Saxon etymology."],"fail_criteria":["Any explicit hard constraint in the prompt is violated.","The response contains unrelated explanation, title, or meta-commentary when output-only text is required."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["unit_test_required","judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L2.002","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L2","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write a 150-220 word original passage about two travelers crossing a dry plain at dusk who encounter a wrecked car. Use a bleak, restrained, desert-gothic narrative voice without naming or imitating any author. Use sparse arid imagery and a detached third-person narrator that does not explain the travelers' inner thoughts. Favor plain diction and several long clauses linked by and or but. Use restrained punctuation; if dialogue appears, write it without quotation marks. Do not mention any real author. Output only the passage.","ground_truth":"A valid response is an original English passage of 150-220 whitespace-delimited words. It must depict two travelers crossing a dry plain at dusk and encountering a wrecked car. Dialogue is optional; if present, it must not use quotation marks. It should use restrained punctuation, sparse arid imagery, detached third-person narration without direct explanation of inner thoughts, and at least two multi-clause sentences linked by coordinating conjunctions.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Output is an original English passage of 150-220 whitespace-delimited words.","It includes two travelers, a dry plain at dusk, and a wrecked car that they encounter.","It does not name a real author.","If dialogue is present, no dialogue line uses quotation marks.",{"style_rubric":{"restrained_punctuation":"0=no evidence or punctuation is ornate/excessive; 1=partly restrained; 2=consistently restrained without impairing readability","sparse_arid_imagery":"0=setting is absent or lush/ornate; 1=some dry-plain imagery; 2=concise, concrete, predominantly arid imagery","detached_narration":"0=frequent explicit explanation of thoughts/emotions; 1=mixed; 2=third-person narration observes action and detail without directly explaining inner states","conjunction_linked_plain_prose":"0=absent; 1=one suitable multi-clause sentence; 2=at least two suitable multi-clause sentences linked by and/or but, with generally plain diction"}},{"legacy_pass_rule":"Pass only if every hard requirement is met and the style-rubric total is at least 6 of 8, with no individual style dimension scored 0."}],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L2.003","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L2","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 150-220 word passage about an older woman in a kitchen recalling her grandmother's hands. Use lyrical, layered prose associated with intergenerational literary fiction, without quoting, closely paraphrasing, or reproducing any existing text. Include all of the following: (1) at least one complete line of direct speech embedded in narration without quotation marks; (2) rhythmic repetition of a word, phrase, or syntactic pattern; (3) at least two concrete sensory or bodily details; and (4) a clear link between the woman's private memory and a wider family, neighborhood, or ancestral memory. Output only the passage.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge_with_required_feature_check","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if all conditions are met: (1) output is 150-220 words and contains only the passage; (2) it centers an older woman in a kitchen recalling her grandmother's hands; (3) it contains at least one complete utterance of direct speech presented without quotation marks and embedded within narration, not merely free indirect discourse; (4) it contains deliberate rhythmic repetition of a word, phrase, or syntactic pattern; (5) it includes at least two specific sensory or bodily images; (6) it explicitly connects the memory to family, neighborhood, community, or ancestral continuity; (7) prose is meaningfully lyrical and layered, evidenced by imagery and syntactic or temporal layering rather than a plain summary; (8) it does not quote or closely paraphrase identifiable existing text. Any missing condition is a fail."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L2.004","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L2","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 150-220 word short passage that evokes the following high-level Calvino-associated traits without quoting or closely reproducing any existing text: lucid present-tense or timeless framing, fable-like geometry, a paradox or recursive idea, and philosophically detached narration. An unnamed traveler arrives at an imaginary city built on a cliff of mirrors. Output only the passage. For length, count words as whitespace-separated tokens.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every required condition is met: (1) 150-220 whitespace-separated words; (2) an unnamed traveler arrives at an imaginary city; (3) the city is explicitly built on, supported by, or inseparably located upon a cliff of mirrors; (4) narration is predominantly present tense or timeless and fable-like; (5) the city contains a clear geometric or structural conceit; (6) the passage contains a recognizable paradox or recursive relation; (7) tone is predominantly detached and reflective rather than confessional or melodramatic; (8) no recognizable quotation or close reproduction of an existing Calvino passage. Do not require exact imitation of Calvino's prose. Fail if any hard condition is absent."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L2.005","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L2","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write a 150-220 word original short scene in the style of Raymond Carver. A couple sit at a small kitchen table in the immediate aftermath of an argument; make that aftermath evident through dialogue, action, or context without explicitly explaining their feelings. Use minimalist diction, restrained dialogue, ordinary domestic detail, emotional withholding, and terse sentence rhythm. Do not quote or reproduce any existing text. Output only the scene, with no title or commentary.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge; a passing response must meet all hard scene, format, and length constraints and satisfy the defined stylistic-feature threshold.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Fail if the response is not 150-220 words; contains a title, commentary, analysis, or other non-scene text; does not depict a couple at a small kitchen table; does not make the immediate aftermath of an argument reasonably evident; or contains an apparent quotation/reproduction of an existing text. For otherwise eligible responses, score each style feature as present/absent: (1) plain, minimally ornamental diction; (2) predominantly short or terse sentence rhythm; (3) restrained, ordinary dialogue where dialogue is used; (4) concrete ordinary domestic detail; (5) emotional withholding rather than explicit explanation of feelings. Pass only if at least 4 of 5 style features are present, including both (1) and (5)."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L3.001","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 320-420 word short story about a librarian who discovers a book whose index changes depending on who reads it. Use calm, dispassionate first- or third-person narration; precise, erudite diction; at least one real or invented scholar, catalogue, apocryphon, or bibliographic citation; and a central metaphysical paradox arising from the changing index. Sustain an austere, learned, labyrinthine mode through the whole story, using recursive, branching, mirrored, or self-referential structures or imagery rather than merely naming such concepts. Avoid sensationalism, imitation labels, commentary, and explanation outside the story. Output only the story.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge: Accept diverse original stories that satisfy every explicit content, length, narration, tone, and sustained-feature requirement; do not require verbatim use of any listed example or a particular plot resolution.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if all conditions hold: (1) the response contains only a self-contained English story, with no analysis, disclaimer, rubric discussion, or imitation label; (2) it is 320-420 words, counted by whitespace-delimited words; (3) a librarian and a book with an index that changes according to the reader are materially present in the plot; (4) the changing index creates or resolves a central metaphysical paradox, not merely a decorative mystery; (5) narration is consistently calm and dispassionate, without melodramatic or sensational framing; (6) precise, learned diction is sustained across the story; (7) it includes at least one meaningful reference to a real or invented scholar, catalogue, apocryphon, or bibliographic source; (8) a labyrinthine or recursive conception is developed semantically through imagery, structure, or plot, such as branching, recursion, mirroring, circularity, or self-reference; (9) the learned, metaphysical, restrained, labyrinthine qualities are sustained in multiple parts of the text rather than supplied by isolated keywords. Fail if any condition is absent. When uncertain whether a feature is central or sustained, fail rather than infer it from isolated words."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["unit_test_required","judge_required","high_difficulty"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L3.002","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 320-420 word piece in a sustained modernist stream-of-consciousness mode associated with Virginia Woolf. A middle-aged woman walks through a London park in the late afternoon. Her consciousness must move between the immediate present and at least three distinct, identifiable memories, each anchored by a concrete sensory detail or event. Throughout, use free-indirect narration with rapid interior shifts, sensory immediacy, peripheral glimpses of at least two minor characters, and an anti-plot structure with no conventional setup-conflict-resolution arc. Use at least one semicolon and at least one parenthetical dash. Maintain the specified voice and techniques throughout rather than only in isolated sentences. Count words by whitespace; hyphenated compounds count as one word. Output only the piece.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if every hard condition is met: (1) 320-420 words under whitespace counting, with hyphenated compounds counted as one word; (2) the present action is a middle-aged woman walking through a London park in late afternoon; (3) the narration uses sustained free-indirect or stream-of-consciousness interior movement; (4) it contains at least three distinct memories, each distinguishable from the others and anchored by a concrete sensory detail or event; (5) sensory immediacy is present in both the current walk and at least one memory; (6) at least two minor characters are glimpsed only peripherally; (7) at least one semicolon and at least one parenthetical dash occur; (8) the prose sustains the requested modernist/Woolf-associated traits throughout, including fluid temporal movement and non-linear sentence rhythm; (9) there is no conventional setup-conflict-climax-resolution plot. Fail if any hard condition is absent. Do not award a pass merely because isolated phrases resemble the target style."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required","high_difficulty"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L3.003","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 320-420 word short scene in English. Use a restrained retrospective literary voice: first-person narration by an aging hotel concierge looking back on a small misunderstanding with a guest that he failed to address at the time. Sustain slightly formal diction, understated emotional repression, gentle self-corrections or hedges, and a preoccupation with duty and professional propriety. Include at least two clear hedges or self-revisions. Output only the scene.","ground_truth":"structured_judge_spec: A passing response is an original English scene of 320-420 words, with no title, explanation, or other text outside the scene. It must sustain first-person retrospective narration by an aging hotel concierge; depict a concrete minor misunderstanding involving a guest that the narrator did not address at the time; use restrained, slightly formal diction; contain at least two clear hedges or self-revisions; convey emotional restraint rather than explicit catharsis; and show concern for duty or professional propriety.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Fail if the response is not an English scene of 320-420 words, contains material outside the scene, lacks a first-person retrospective narrator who is an aging hotel concierge, lacks a guest-related misunderstanding that was left unaddressed at the time, or lacks at least two identifiable hedges/self-revisions. For remaining style criteria, pass only if the narration is predominantly restrained and slightly formal, emotion is conveyed indirectly or suppressed rather than openly dramatized, and professional duty/propriety materially motivates the narrator's inaction or reflection. Do not require imitation of any named author."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required","high_difficulty"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L3.004","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 320-420 word short piece in a Latin American magical-realist mode, without naming or imitating any specific author. A small inland town wakes one morning to discover that its river has reversed direction. Throughout, report the impossible event with matter-of-fact gravity; use predominantly long, rolling sentences; include rich, concrete sensory details of small-town life; present both a multi-generational perspective and a communal narrator; and make the reversed river central to the piece. Output only the piece.","ground_truth":"stylistic_judge","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if all conditions are met: (a) the response contains 320-420 words and only the requested literary piece, with no prefatory/explanatory/meta text; (b) the river reversing direction is central to the narrative; (c) the event is narrated in a matter-of-fact, grave magical-realist mode rather than as a puzzle, dream explanation, or scientific report; (d) the prose predominantly uses extended, flowing sentence structures; (e) it includes multiple concrete sensory details of inland small-town life; (f) it contains both an explicit multi-generational perspective and a communal narrator, such as first-person plural narration or a clearly collective town voice; (g) these features are sustained across the piece rather than appearing only once. Do not require a named character unless the prompt explicitly does so."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required","high_difficulty"]}
{"probe_id":"instr.style_emulation.L3.005","canonical_id":"instr.style_emulation","category":"instr","level":"L3","axis_values":{"style_specificity":"named_author"},"prompt":"Write an original 320-420 word literary-nonfiction piece in a distinct voice. A first-person observer watches a winter storm approach across a wide field, and the description widens into natural-historical and metaphysical reflection. Include precise natural observation, at least one movement from a concrete detail to reflection on vast scale, time, or existence, and a mix of one brief aphoristic sentence with longer reflective sentences. Convey awe without sentimentality. Do not imitate, name, or claim the style of any author. Output only the piece.","ground_truth":"constraint_judge: Accept an original 320-420 word piece that sustains first-person observation of a winter storm crossing a wide field; includes at least two concrete natural or meteorological details; includes at least one explicit transition from a concrete observation to reflection on time, scale, existence, or cosmology; includes at least one declarative sentence of 10 words or fewer; conveys restrained awe rather than overtly sentimental language; and does not name, imitate, or claim the voice of a specific author.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Pass only if all of the following are met: (1) the response contains 320-420 words, counted by whitespace-delimited tokens; (2) it is an original first-person literary-nonfiction piece; (3) a winter storm approaching or moving across a wide field is clearly present; (4) it contains at least two concrete natural or meteorological observations; (5) it explicitly links at least one concrete observation to time, scale, existence, or cosmology; (6) it contains at least one declarative sentence of 10 words or fewer; (7) its emotional register is restrained awe, without direct sentimental declarations such as nostalgia, tenderness, or personal consolation; (8) it neither names a target author nor claims, requests, or performs imitation of a specific author's voice. Fail if any condition is absent. No numeric threshold is used."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["judge_required","high_difficulty"]}
